2026-10-17  Joe McCabe <joe@jmccabe.org>

        * Bug fix: fix .htaccess plugin crashes on dynamic .htaccess rewrite
          under load and on Linux/64-bit directive parsing and authentication
          (Oracle 14094667, 6928578).

            The .htaccess plugin (src/server/plugins/htaccess/) and related
            file-buffer / authentication helpers in src/server/base/buffer.cpp,
            src/server/libaccess/cryptwrapper.cpp, and src/server/safs/auth.cpp
            had two classes of crash defects:
            - Dynamic .htaccess / password / group file updates under load
              (Oracle 14094667): htaccess_cfg_open(), htaccess_init_group(),
              htaccess_userdb(), simple_user(), and simple_group() opened files
              with filebuf_open(), which memory-maps files via mmap().  When a
              .htaccess, .htpasswd, or group file was truncated/rewritten in
              place (O_TRUNC) while concurrent worker threads were reading
              buf->fp, the kernel delivered SIGBUS on any access past the new
              EOF.  These readers now use filebuf_open_nostat_nommap() /
              filebuf_close_nommap(), and filebuf_open_nostat_nommap() has been
              updated to read until EOF (dynamically growing its heap buffer if
              the file grew after stat() and succeeding on short reads if the
              file shrank or was truncated concurrently).
            - Linux and 64-bit directive/auth crashes and memory corruption
              (Oracle 6928578):
              * ACL_CryptCompare() in cryptwrapper.cpp passed the return value
                of crypt() directly to strcmp() without checking for NULL
                (modern Linux glibc/libxcrypt returns NULL on invalid or empty
                salts), and set_password() in htpasswd.c left salt[2]
                uninitialized before calling crypt(pw, salt).
              * htaccess_evaluate_access() allocated len + 1 bytes for path and
                root_dir, causing a 1-byte heap buffer overflow on directory
                requests when htaccess_strcpy_dir() appended a trailing '/'.
              * _htaccess_freectxt() freed ctxt->user_check_fn,
                ctxt->group_check_fn, ctxt->auth_grplist, and
                ctxt->auth_grplfile inside a for (x = 0; x < ctxt->num_sec; x++)
                loop (double-freeing whenever 2 or more .htaccess files applied
                in a directory hierarchy) and called FREE(ctxt->sec) without
                freeing the head placeholder's allow/deny/auth arrays.
              * htaccess_parse_access_dir() did not propagate syntax/OOM errors
                to htaccess_evaluate_access(), leaked sec on early error
                returns, failed to reset the active method mask on </Limit> and
                </LimitExcept>, did not strip trailing whitespace on order
                directives, and passed args=1 instead of 2 for PASS2 registered
                directives.
              * htaccess_check_auth() left user_fn and group_fn uninitialized on
                custom-auth paths, called strcmp(NULL, group_db) when
                ctxt->auth_pwfile was NULL, and leaked STRDUP("basic") /
                STRDUP("unspecified") into ctxt->auth_type / ctxt->auth_name.
              * htaccess_in_ip() and htaccess_check_allow() mutated
                sec->allow[y] / sec->deny[y] in place, used 64-bit unsigned
                long for IPv4 addresses/masks, and masked allowedip before
                calling inet_addr().
              * simple_group() in auth.cpp used strncmp(user, t, u - t) without
                checking strlen(user) == (size_t)(u - t) (prefix-matching
                usernames), allocated MALLOC(buf->len) once without dynamic
                growth, and omitted the last line if it lacked a trailing
                newline.

        * Bug fix: fix FastCGI stub process (Fastcgistub) crashes, concurrency
          races, and memory safety defects across spawn, restart, and shutdown
          (Oracle 12643441).

            Fastcgistub and the FastCGI NSAPI plugin in src/server/plugins/fastcgi/
            contained multiple lifecycle and memory-safety bugs:
            - stopStubThread() invoked handleExit() (which destroyed and nulled
              lockProcessList) while handleExit() was also registered via
              atexit(), causing a second handleExit() invocation on process exit
              to pass NULL to PR_Lock(lockProcessList) and crash with SIGSEGV.
            - cleanStub() invoked stopStubThread() (acquiring locks, joining
              threads, deleting process objects, and closing files) directly
              inside the SIGTERM signal handler, and serveRequests() called
              PR_Close(connFd) on the connFd == NULL branch.  cleanStub() now
              only sets an atomic stop flag, serveRequests() polls stubFd with
              a bounded timeout so SIGTERM wakes the accept loop cleanly on the
              main thread, and handleExit() is idempotent.
            - Static PR_NewLock() initializers ran before cleanupEnvironment()
              closed inherited descriptors; locks are now initialized in main()
              after cleanupEnvironment(), and fd-close loops in both
              cleanupEnvironment() and FcgiProcess::execFcgiProgram() use
              close_range() on Linux with a 65536 fallback cap.
            - FcgiProcess left createRequest uninitialized, handleStartRequest()
              deleted proc on duplicate RQ_START while processRequest() read
              fcgiProcess->createRequest after processMessage() returned
              (use-after-free), and childExitHandlerThread() raced with
              processRequest() on closing connfd.  Ownership of proc and connfd
              is now explicitly transferred via keepConnOpen only when a new
              process is queued for childExitHandlerThread().
            - processRequest() compared the single-read byte count readlen
              rather than the cumulative iobufLen against reqMsgLen when framing
              multi-chunk requests, and loaded untrusted reqMsgType wire values
              through a C++ enum type.
            - parseRequest() required RQT_ARGV0 on RQ_OVERLOAD and
              RQ_VS_SHUTDOWN requests (which StubExec::buildServerRequest() does
              not send), pre-allocated a non-NULL empty environment array even
              when RQT_ENVP was absent (wiping the child environment via
              execve()), and leaked partially populated entries when
              unbuildEnvArray() resized.
            - Fixed allocator mismatches (free() vs delete on sockaddr structs,
              PL_strfree() vs delete on vsIdList strings, new vs PR_NEW on
              ChildProcessData, delete[] vs delete in CircularBuffer), missing
              NULL guards on logFd / fcgiStubLogDir / strrchr(stubBindPath, '_'),
              1-byte under-allocation of pluginDir in nsapi_module_init(),
              chRoot/chDir copy-constructor leaks/aliasing and operator== result
              discard in FcgiServerConfig, double-close of writePipe and leaked
              requestMsg in StubExec, 32-bit file-size/Content-Length/rlimit
              truncation in FcgiRequest/FilterRole/FcgiProcess, and missing
              $ORIGIN RPATH on Fastcgistub.

        * Bug fix: fix PAM conversation response allocation and free symmetry on
          password-aging and account-expiry paths (Oracle 6911225).

            converse() in src/server/libaccess/pamauth.c previously assigned the
            string literal "" to reply[i].resp for every PAM message style.  When
            password aging or account expiration caused pam_acct_mgmt() (or
            pam_authenticate()) to invoke the conversation callback with
            PAM_TEXT_INFO or PAM_ERROR_MSG messages, the PAM framework's
            free_resp() routine attempted to free() the read-only literal and
            crashed webservd.  pamauth_authenticate() now passes the supplied
            password through pam_conv.appdata_ptr, and converse() leaves
            reply[i].resp as NULL for informational, error, and echo-on messages
            while duplicating the password with strdup() only for
            PAM_PROMPT_ECHO_OFF prompts (cleaning up any partial allocations on
            error).  pamauth_get_groups_for_user() and pamauth_userexists() also
            now guard against negative sysconf(_SC_GETPW_R_SIZE_MAX) returns,
            check the return value of util_getpwnam(), and free the passwd
            buffer on allocation failure.

        * New feature: enable PAM authentication (auth-db url="pam") on Linux.

            FEAT_PAM=1 was previously enabled only on Solaris in
            make/defines_WebServer.mk because src/server/libaccess/pamauth.c
            relied on Solaris-specific PAM message constants (PAM_MSG_NOCONF,
            PAM_CONV_INTERRUPT), non-const struct pam_message ** conversation
            signatures, and the Solaris-private _getgroupsbymember() libc
            routine.  pamauth.c now uses Linux-PAM compatible conversation
